    </page>
    <page component="ham-not-soln">
      <p>
        <ol>
          <li>
            <p>
              The graph <m>\bfG_{1}</m> is hamiltonian.
              One example of a hamiltonian cycle is <m>1,5,7,3,2,6,8,9,10,4</m>.
              (There are others.)
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <p>
              The graph <m>\bfG_{2}</m> is not hamiltonian.
              One reason for this is that removing the vertex <m>2</m> leaves a disconnected graph.
              Put another way, <m>1,10,4,2</m> forms a cycle, and the only way to get from the other vertices onto that cycle is via vertex <m>2</m>.
              Once you're onto it, there's no way off again.
              Thus, there's no way to construct a cycle containing all <m>10</m> vertices.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <p>
              The graph <m>\bfG_{3}</m> is hamiltonian.
              One example of a hamiltonian cycle is <m>1,5,6,2,3,8,9,10,4,7</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <p>
              The graph <m>\bfG_{4}</m> is not hamiltonian.
              This is a nontrivial fact.
              For those interested in a complete proof, see the end of this document.
              (You will not be tested on the proof, so it's purely optional reading.)
            </p>
          </li>
          
          <li>
            <p>
              The graph <m>\bfG_{5}</m> (formed by adding one edge to the Petersen graph <m>\bfG_{1}</m>) is hamiltonian.
              One example of a hamiltonian cycle is <m>10,7,1,5,8,4,6,3,2,9</m>.
            </p>
          </li>
        </ol>
      </p>
      
      
      <proof>
        <title>Proof the Petersen Graph is not hamiltonian</title>
        
        <p>
          Consider what a hamiltonian cycle in <m>\bfG_{4}</m> would look like.
          The graph has <m>15</m> edges, and a hamiltonian cycle would use <m>10</m> of them.
          Thus, you'd have the cycle and five leftover edges that would connect vertices across the cycle (<q>chords</q>).
          How would these edges fit along the cycle? If an edge connected a vertex to a vertex two steps down the cycle (for example, if the cycle contains the sequence <m>a,b,c</m> of vertices and there's an edge from <m>a</m> to <m>c</m>), then there would be a triangle (a cyle on three vertices) in the graph.
          There is no such cycle in <m>\bfG_{4}</m>.
          If a chord connects a vertex to another vertex at distance three along the hamiltonian cycle, then there is a cycle on four vertices.
          However, there is no such cycle in <m>\bfG_{4}</m>.
        </p>
        
        <p>
          Now suppose that a chord connects a vertex <m>v_{0}</m> to another vertex <m>v_{1}</m> at distance four along the hamiltonian cycle.
          Let <m>v_{0}'</m> be the vertex opposite <m>v_{0}</m> along the hamiltonian cycle.
          There is also a chord incident with <m>v_{0}'</m>, connecting it across the hamiltonian cycle to a vertex <m>v_{1}'</m>.
          We've already explained that the distance from <m>v_{0}'</m> to <m>v_{1}'</m> along the hamiltonian cycle cannot be less than four, and it cannot be five since that would give <m>v_{0}'</m> degree four.
          Thus the distance along the hamiltonian cycle from <m>v_{0}'</m> to <m>v_{1}'</m> must be four.
          Now <m>v_{0},v_{1},v_{1}',v_{0}'</m> is a cycle of length four (draw a picture to convince yourself), which we don't have in <m>\bfG_{4}</m>.
          Finally, the only option that remains is that the chords all connect vertices that are exactly opposite each other on the hamiltonian cycle.
          However, then if <m>v_{0}</m> and <m>v_{1}</m> are adjacent along the hamiltonian cycle and <m>v_{0}'</m> and <m>v_{1}'</m> are the vertices opposite them, <m>v_{0},v_{1},v_{1}',v_{0}'</m> is a cycle of length four.
        </p>
      </proof>
    </page>
  </worksheet>
  <worksheet xml:id="ws-triangle-free" component="graphs-triangle-free">
    <title>Graphs with <m>\omega(\bfG)=2</m> and <m>\chi(\bfG)</m> large</title>
    <page>
      <p>
        We've already seen that the chromatic number <m>\chi(\bfG)</m> of a graph <m>\bfG</m> is at least its clique number <m>\omega(\bfG)</m>. (Recall that if <m>\omega(\bfG)=t</m>, then <m>\bfG</m> contains a subgraph isomorphic to <m>\bfK_{t}</m> and <m>\chi(\bfK_{t})=t</m>.) In this activity, we're going to see how to construct graphs with <m>\omega(\bfG)=2</m> and <m>\chi(\bfG)=t</m> for any integer <m>t\geq 2</m>. We will focus only on the construction of Mycielski, which is one of two constructions included in the text.
      </p>
      <p>
        This construction aims to create a graph <m>\bfG_{t}</m> with <m>\chi(\bfG_t)=t</m> and <m>\omega(\bfG_{t})=2</m>. The construction is inductive. It starts by creating <m>\bfG_{3}</m>, then uses that to make <m>\bfG_{4}</m>, which it uses to make <m>\bfG_{5}</m>, etc. As you go through, discuss the steps with your group members. The questions in <em>italics</em> are questions you must be able to answer to ensure you understand as you go through things.
      </p>
      <exercise workspace="0.5cm">
        <statement>
          <p>
            We proceed by induction on <m>t</m>. For <m>t=3</m>, we take <m>\bfG_{3}</m> to be the cycle <m>\bfC_{5}</m> on five vertices. (<em>Why is <m>\chi(\bfG_{3})=3</m>? Why is <m>\omega(\bfG_{3})=2</m>?</em>)
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="0.5cm">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Now assume that for some <m>t\ge3</m>, we have determined the graph <m>\bfG_{t}</m>. Suppose that <m>\bfG_{t}</m> has <m>n_{t}</m> vertices. Label the vertices of <m>\bfG_{t}</m> as <m>x_{1},x_{2},\dots,x_{n_t}</m>. Construct <m>\bfG_{t+1}</m> as follows.
            <ol marker="a.">
              <li>
                <p>
                  Begin with an independent set <m>I</m> of cardinality <m>n_{t}</m>.
                  (<em>What is an independent set?</em>)
                </p>
              </li>
              
              <li>
                <p>
                  Label the points of <m>I</m> as <m>y_{1},y_{2},\dots,y_{n_t}</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              
              <li>
                <p>
                  Add a copy of <m>\bfG_{t}</m> with <m>y_{i}</m> adjacent to <m>x_{j}</m> if and only if <m>x_{i}</m> is adjacent to <m>x_{j}</m>.
                </p>
              </li>
              
              <li>
                <p>
                  Attach a new vertex <m>z</m> adjacent to all vertices in <m>I</m> (but none from the copy of <m>\bfG_{t}</m>).
                </p>
              </li>
            </ol>
          </p>
          <p>
            <em>How many vertices does <m>\bfG_{t+1}</m> contain?</em> (Your formula will contain <m>n_{t}</m>.) You should be able to draw <m>\bfG_{4}</m> by hand, and <m>\bfG_{5}</m> will have <m>23</m> vertices.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <!-- </page>
      <page> -->
      <exercise>
        <title><m>\omega(\bfG_{t+1})=2</m></title>
        <introduction>
          
          
          <p>
            To see that <m>\omega(\bfG_{t+1})=2</m>, it will suffice to argue that <m>\bfG_{t+1}</m> contains no triangle (<m>\bfK_{3}</m>).
            To do this, we consider where the vertices of a triangle would be:
          </p>
        </introduction>
        
        
        
        <task workspace="0.5cm">
          <p>
            Explain why any triangle in <m>\bfG_{t+1}</m> must contain a vertex of <m>I\cup\{z\}</m>.
          </p>
        </task>
        
        <task workspace="0.5cm">
          <p>
            Explain why any triangle in <m>\bfG_{t+1}</m> contains only one vertex of <m>I</m>.
          </p>
        </task>
        
        <task workspace="0.5cm">
          <p>
            Explain why a triangle cannot contain a vertex of <m>I</m> and the vertex <m>z</m>.
          </p>
        </task>
        
        <task>
          <p>
            A triangle must thus contain a vertex <m>y_{i}\in I</m> and vertices <m>x_{j}</m> and <m>x_{k}</m> from the copy of <m>\bfG_{t}</m>.
            Explain why <m>\{y_{i},x_{j},x_{k}\}</m> forming a triangle mean that there's a triangle in <m>\bfG_{t}</m>.
          </p>
        </task>
        
      </exercise>
    </page>
    <page>
      <exercise>
        <title><m>\chi(\bfG_{t+1})=t+1</m></title>
        <task workspace="1cm">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <em>What part of the construction ensures that <m>\chi(\bfG_{t+1})\ge t</m>?</em>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="2cm">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <em>How can you color the vertices of <m>I</m> using <m>t</m> colors?</em> (Hint: Pair vertices of <m>I</m> with vertices of <m>\bfG_{t}</m>.) <em>What color does <m>z</m> get? How many colors have you used and what does this say about <m>\chi(\bfG_{t+1})</m>?</em>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="2cm">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Try coloring <m>\bfG_{t+1}</m> using only <m>t</m> colors. Suppose that <m>\phi</m> is a proper coloring of <m>\bfG_{t+1}</m> using the colors <m>\{1,2,\dots,t\}</m>. It's safe to assume that <m>\phi(z) = t</m> (reshuffle the colors otherwise). Let <m>S</m> be the set of vertices in the copy of <m>\bfG_{t}</m> to which <m>\phi</m> assigns color <m>t</m>. <em>Can the set <m>S</m> be empty? Explain your reasoning.</em>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1.5cm">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Since <m>\phi(z)=t</m>, <em>what do you know about the set (or even number) of colors used on <m>I</m>?</em>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1.5cm">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <em>Why can you change the color of each <m>x_{i}\in S</m> to match the color <m>\phi</m> assigns to <m>y_{i}</m> and maintain a proper coloring?</em>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1cm">
          <statement>
            <p>
              <em>Now how many colors have you used on the copy of <m>\bfG_{t}</m>?</em>
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
      </exercise>
    </page>
  </worksheet>
  <worksheet xml:id="ws-graphs-planar" component="graphs-planar">
    <title>Euler's Polyhedral Formula</title>
    <!-- <page> -->
    <p>It turns out that you could draw hundreds of connected planar graphs
      and keep finding the same pattern that arises in the platonic solids:</p>
      
      <theorem>
        <title>Euler's Formula</title>
        
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>\bfG</m> be a connected planar graph with <m>n</m> vertices and <m>m</m> edges.
            Then every planar drawing of <m>\bfG</m> has <m>f</m> faces, where <m>f</m> satisfies <m>n-m+f=2</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </theorem>
      
      <exercise workspace="0.35in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Notice that Euler's Formula requires that <m>\bfG</m> be a <term>connected</term> planar graph.
            Find a counterexample to show that the formula <m>n-m+f=2</m> does not hold if <m>\bfG</m> is a planar graph that is not connected.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise workspace="0.15in">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Since <m>\bfG</m> must be connected, we start by considering the minimal case in which <m>\bfG</m> has no cycles (but for the general number <m>n</m> of vertices).
            What type of graph does that mean <m>\bfG</m> is? How many edges does <m>\bfG</m> have (in terms of <m>n</m>)? (In future questions, I'll call this value of <m>m</m> by the name <m>m_{0}</m>.) Verify Euler's formula in this case.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <task workspace="0.7in">
          <p>
            Draw a planar drawing of a connected planar graph <m>\bfG</m> with with <m>n=7</m> or <m>n=8</m> vertices and more than <m>m_{0}</m> edges. Make sure your graph has at least one vertex of degree <m>1</m>.
          </p>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find <m>n</m>, <m>m</m>, and <m>f</m> for your graph.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.25in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Is there an edge <m>e</m> you can erase from your graph and leave a graph <m>\bfG'</m> that is still connected? What determines whether or not an edge is safe to remove while still leaving a connected graph?
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.15in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Erase an edge <m>e</m> from <m>\bfG</m> to give you a planar drawing of a connected planar graph <m>\bfG'</m>.
              Let <m>n'</m>, <m>m'</m>, and <m>f'</m> be the number of vertices, edges, and faces respectively of <m>\bfG'</m>.
              What are <m>n',m',f'</m> for your graph?
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Find equations that relate <m>n'</m>, <m>m'</m>, and <m>f'</m> to <m>n</m>, <m>m</m>, and <m>f</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
      </exercise>
      <!-- </page>
      <page> -->
      
      <exercise>
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Now let's suppose that <m>\bfG</m> is a connected planar graph with <m>n</m> vertices and <m>m</m> edges where <m>m\gt m_{0}</m>.
            Suppose that Euler's formula holds for any connected planar graph with fewer than <m>m</m> edges.
            (What type of proof are we setting up here?)
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task workspace="0.25in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Why is there an edge <m>e</m> you can delete from <m>\bfG</m> to produce a connected planar graph <m>\bfG'</m>?
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.25in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Let <m>n'</m>, <m>m'</m>, and <m>f'</m> be the number of vertices, edges, and faces respectively of <m>\bfG'</m>.
              What are <m>n',m',f'</m> in terms of <m>n</m>, <m>m</m>, and <m>f</m>?
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.35in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              What equation involving <m>n'</m>, <m>m'</m>, and <m>f'</m> must hold? Why?
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.35in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Prove that <m>n-m+f=2</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <introduction>
          <p>
            In the grand scheme of things, Euler's formula doesn't seem that useful for determining if a graph is planar or not.
            However, we can deduce something useful from it.
            In this problem, we're going to imagine a fixed planar drawing of a connected planar graph <m>\bfG</m> and let <m>n</m>, <m>m</m>, and <m>f</m> be the number of vertices, edges, and faces, respectively, in our planar drawing.
            We'll assume that <m>n\geq 4</m> to avoid some annoying things.
          </p>
          
          <p>
            Let <m>S = \set{(e,F)\mid e\text{ is an edge of }\bfG\text{ and part of the boundary of face }F}</m>.
          </p>
          
        </introduction>
        <task workspace="0.15in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              If I pick an edge <m>e_{1}</m> of <m>\bfG</m>, what is the largest possible number of faces that <m>e_{1}</m> can be part of the boundary of? So what does that tell us about the number of ordered pairs in <m>S</m> that have <m>e_{1}</m> as the first coordinate?
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.15in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Use the previous part to find an inequality involving <m>m</m> and <m>|S|</m> (the size of <m>S</m>).
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.15in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              If I pick a face <m>F_{1}</m> of <m>\bfG</m>, what is the smallest possible number of edges that can be used to form the boundary of <m>F_{1}</m>?
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.15in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Use the previous part to find an inequality involving <m>f</m> and <m>|S|</m> (the size of <m>S</m>).
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.15in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Combine two previous parts to get an inequality involving <m>m</m> and <m>f</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.15in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Using Euler's formula and the previous part, show that <m>m\leq 3n-6</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.15in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Prove that <m>\bfK_{5}</m> is not planar.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
      </exercise>
      <!-- </page>
      <page> -->
      <exercise>
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Recall that the complete bipartite graph <m>\bfK_{3,3}</m> is the bipartite graph with two independent sets each of size three and all the possible edges (nine in total) between vertices in different independent sets.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task workspace="1.5in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Can you make a planar drawing of <m>\bfK_{3,3}</m>? If not, what does the <m>m\leq 3n-6</m> restriction tell you here?
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.5in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Notice that since <m>\bfK_{3,3}</m> does not contain any vertices of degree <m>1</m>, every face of a planar drawing would be bounded by a cycle, and thus every edge is part of the boundary of two faces.
              Since <m>\bfK_{3,3}</m> is bipartite, what can you say about the length of every cycle in <m>\bfK_{3,3}</m>?
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.25in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              As in the previous problem, count edge-face pairs.
              How many pairs must there be for <m>\bfK_{3,3}</m>?
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="0.35in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              Let <m>f_{4}</m> be the number of faces bounded by a cycle of length <m>4</m> and let <m>f_{6}</m> be the number of faces bounded by a cycle of length <m>6</m>. Express the total number of faces <m>f</m> in terms of <m>f_{4}</m> and <m>f_{6}</m>. Then express the number of edge-face pairs in terms of <m>f_{4}</m> and <m>f_{6}</m>.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task workspace="1in">
          <statement>
            <p>
              What does Euler's formula tell you <m>f</m> would have to be for <m>\bfK_{3,3}</m>? What lower bound does this give you for the number of edge-face pairs, and how does this show that <m>\bfK_{3,3}</m> is not planar?
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
      </exercise>
      <exercise>
        <statement>
          <p>
            For each graph on the next page, find a planar drawing or
            give a reason why the graph is not planar.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
      <!-- </page> -->
      
      
    </worksheet>
